@@ -111,16 +87,20 @@
 GSN Inversion is a method for view synthesis and scene completion that inverts source views into a latent code grid to locally condition a trained radiance field. It solves the problem of generating unseen parts of a scene or editing scenes by optimizing latent codes rather than sampling from the prior. Unlike methods relying on global coordinate systems, GSN uses a local coordinate system that is robust to rearrangements of latent codes, such as 2D rotations, whereas global systems suffer rendering quality degradation as rotation angles increase.
 
+### Hybrid GAN Inversion
+A hybrid inversion scheme where an encoder produces an initial guess of the latent code and pose, which are then refined for a small number of iterations [S2]. Feed a real image into the encoder E to predict an initial latent code w and a canonical map [S2]. Convert the predicted canonical map to a point cloud [S2]. Run a Perspective-n-Point (PnP) solver on the point cloud to recover all pose parameters (view matrix and focal length) [S2]. Split the initial latent code w into a different vector for each layer, referred to as $w^+ \in W^+$ [S2]. For a fixed number of steps N, update $w^+$ and the pose to minimize reconstruction error between rendered and input images [S2]. N: Number of optimization steps, typically as few as 10 [S2]. Unlike methods initializing from average/random latent codes requiring hundreds of steps, this method accelerates convergence [S2].
